WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.069 --> 00:00:02.873
[bike engine roaring]

00:00:02.873 --> 00:00:04.950
[tires screeching]

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.860
To go that fast with that much lean angle,

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.910
that's basically an immediate crash.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:10.350
Hi, my name's Casey Stoner.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.198
I'm a former MotoGP world champion.

00:00:12.198 --> 00:00:13.672
In my career, I managed to win

00:00:13.672 --> 00:00:15.272
two MotoGP world championships.

00:00:15.272 --> 00:00:17.746
Today, we're looking at
motorcycle scenes from movies

00:00:17.746 --> 00:00:19.238
and judging how real they are.

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:29.220
Motorcycle with one hand
isn't difficult at all,

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:32.260
but with your opposite
hand, it is very difficult.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.030
As soon as you look around or look back,

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:35.570
quite often you veer offline.

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:37.700
And there's people that can
ride and do wheel stands

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.260
and stoppies while riding
backwards and sidesaddle

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.278
and all that sort of other thing,

00:00:42.278 --> 00:00:43.730
but it takes a heck of a lot of practice,

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:45.080
and it's not at those speeds

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:46.771
while trying to sword fight.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:56.600
Yeah, there's not a whole
lot of realism in that one.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.420
Trying to get something through a wheel

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:00.650
when it's spinning that
fast is nearly impossible.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:02.140
If anything happens to the back wheel,

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:04.690
it certainly doesn't cause
the front to come up and go.

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:07.174
Now, if something gets
lodged in the front wheel,

00:01:07.174 --> 00:01:08.007
that will happen.

00:01:08.007 --> 00:01:10.007
The back will come up
and go over the front.

00:01:17.531 --> 00:01:19.098
It was quite realistic.

00:01:19.098 --> 00:01:20.364
Wet white lines,

00:01:20.364 --> 00:01:22.350
the way we explain it in
racing, it's like ice.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:23.860
As soon as you come across a white line

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.261
that's not painted with a special grip,

00:01:26.261 --> 00:01:27.323
it's literally just,

00:01:27.323 --> 00:01:29.330
as soon as you touch it,
you're pretty much gone.

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:30.470
If you know you're going to crash

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:31.803
or you know you're going to hit something,

00:01:31.803 --> 00:01:33.003
a lot of the time, the best way

00:01:33.003 --> 00:01:34.876
is either just to lay the bike down,

00:01:34.876 --> 00:01:37.110
because a low side or a front-end lose,

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:38.581
there's going to be a
lot less complications.

00:01:38.581 --> 00:01:39.660
When you have a high side,

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.180
which is when you lose the rear end

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:42.906
and then it creates grip again

00:01:42.906 --> 00:01:44.514
and it flicks you over the high

00:01:44.514 --> 00:01:46.930
and basically sends you up in the air.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:49.054
So when you go, what
goes up must come down,

00:01:49.054 --> 00:01:51.120
and it's a lot more painful,

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.230
and I definitely had a little
more experience than I'd like.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.627
I would say probably a 2.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.829
Eyes would be watering, that's for sure.

00:02:04.829 --> 00:02:06.600
The no-helmet thing would be

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.360
probably the most difficult aspect.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:09.751
Any little bits of dust,

00:02:09.751 --> 00:02:11.220
of rock flicking up off a car,

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:12.210
off the bike in front,

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.285
almost impossible, a lot
of the stuff he does.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:20.290
We do that basically to get a feeling

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:21.410
of where the ground is.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:22.625
It gives us an understanding

00:02:22.625 --> 00:02:24.983
of where our lean angles
are and things like that.

00:02:24.983 --> 00:02:28.290
Now, without a knee slider,
it's doing very little,

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:30.348
other than to take a bit of skin off.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:35.950
I've seen bikes explode.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:37.890
As these are the kind
of standard road bikes,

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:39.974
the temperatures are so high,

00:02:39.974 --> 00:02:42.374
especially around the head of the exhaust,

00:02:42.374 --> 00:02:45.296
that it really can ignite quite a lot.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:51.170
So, the shifting is something
to be brought up a little.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:53.117
You don't need to do a clutch.

00:02:53.117 --> 00:02:55.500
That's a very, very old system

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.410
to basically ease the bike
in a little bit easier

00:02:58.410 --> 00:02:59.910
and a little bit smoother into gear.

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:02.420
Unless you're going back
gears going into a corner,

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:03.560
you don't need to use the clutch.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:04.990
On modern bikes these days,

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:07.026
we have something called
a quickshifter now.

00:03:07.026 --> 00:03:08.980
So it cuts the power to
the engine for a second,

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:12.350
allows the bike to reduce
a little bit of torque

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:15.992
so that the gear can select
and then they can keep going.

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:22.536
The crash was pretty fine.

00:03:22.536 --> 00:03:24.367
You know, losing the front
end in that situation,

00:03:24.367 --> 00:03:26.810
I guarantee he would have a
little bit of meat missing

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:28.810
down the side of his body.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:29.960
As soon as you touch tarmac,

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.540
it likes to rip apart whatever you've got,

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:33.770
even with leathers on.

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:36.890
But overall, because he slid
onto the gravel as well,

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:37.940
it would have been a little softer

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:39.960
than sliding the whole way on tarmac.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:42.752
I'd probably rate this scene around an 8.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:49.680
Of course. There's
always a key in the bike.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:51.400
You know, they go and
get on and just start it,

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:52.233
and off it goes.

00:03:57.239 --> 00:03:59.638
I mean, going up stairs on a bike

00:03:59.638 --> 00:04:01.340
realistically isn't very hard at all.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:04.360
Even for myself, who's not
a technical rider like this.

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:06.271
They actually have that as part of a race,

00:04:06.271 --> 00:04:07.420
going up them and down them,

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:09.287
and they're actually
racing through the streets.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.160
Riding across the top of that small gap

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:19.040
would have been the most difficult
part of that whole scene.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.160
As easy as it seems, when
you're going at that speed,

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:23.410
one little mistake can
be quite catastrophic.

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:26.342
So getting those gaps
would have been definitely

00:04:26.342 --> 00:04:27.630
a little bit tricky at those speeds.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:28.971
You know, they were doing
a very, very good job

00:04:28.971 --> 00:04:29.804
on that scene.

00:04:34.316 --> 00:04:36.920
It's not that hard to
do those sort of drops.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:39.764
Actually, the faster you
go, the better it is.

00:04:39.764 --> 00:04:42.930
The slower you go, the more
straight down you sort of go.

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:45.963
But you come down and you land flat

00:04:45.963 --> 00:04:47.490
from too high of a point,

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:49.380
you can break your
wrists, break your ankles.

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:51.021
If you hit the seat as well,

00:04:51.021 --> 00:04:52.596
people have broken their backs and things,

00:04:52.596 --> 00:04:55.060
so it can be quite tricky.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:56.990
You know, those drops were realistic,

00:04:56.990 --> 00:04:57.982
you can do that,

00:04:57.982 --> 00:04:59.970
but, yeah, it starts
getting bigger than that,

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:01.747
then some injuries start to happen.

00:05:01.747 --> 00:05:02.910
It would probably be a 9.

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:04.670
Pretty incredible what
people can do on bikes,

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:07.062
especially these days. They
just keep pushing the limits.

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:13.340
They actually had a key this time!

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:14.223
They got it right with that.

00:05:14.223 --> 00:05:15.682
They needed a key to start the bike.

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:22.100
Jumping off the truck
might've been possible

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:24.430
with a motocross bike or an off-road bike,

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:26.730
but with a road bike
with two people on it,

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:27.780
as soon as they landed,

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:29.040
it just would have bounced them

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.443
and high sided or something.

00:05:31.443 --> 00:05:34.393
That basically is nearly
impossible for that to happen.

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:40.546
The burnout, turning around,

00:05:40.546 --> 00:05:41.595
yes, that's possible

00:05:41.595 --> 00:05:43.670
the way they were just
sitting there and doing it.

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:45.160
Especially with two people on board,

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:46.670
you've got a lot of
weight over the rear tire.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:48.700
That's quite hard to then
get it to spin around.

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:51.210
A lot of the whole burnouts
and then taking off from there

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:52.847
basically being like a boost,

00:05:52.847 --> 00:05:54.870
that's not the case.
It's a negative for us.

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:57.080
If you've ever seen somebody do burnouts

00:05:57.080 --> 00:05:58.620
and you have a look at
that tire afterwards,

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:01.330
there's a big flat part of the tire,

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:03.464
because you've basically just
melted the rubber off it.

00:06:03.464 --> 00:06:04.430
And we don't want it flat.

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:05.863
We need to keep a round surface.

00:06:05.863 --> 00:06:08.604
With cars, you kind of do
need to do a burnout sometimes

00:06:08.604 --> 00:06:10.513
to try and heat the tire through.

00:06:12.066 --> 00:06:14.733
[siren wailing]

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:17.178
A lot of movies get this wrong,

00:06:17.178 --> 00:06:18.236
but this one in particular,

00:06:18.236 --> 00:06:20.800
is the fact that cars can
just catch them so quickly.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:22.416
There's very few supercars

00:06:22.416 --> 00:06:25.682
that can go as quick as what bikes can.

00:06:25.682 --> 00:06:27.471
Especially a Ducati.

00:06:27.471 --> 00:06:29.600
You know, that's top of
the line at the time.

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:32.100
Bikes have acceleration that's incredible.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:36.025
You know, it's basically
a 160-, 180-kilo bike

00:06:36.025 --> 00:06:37.991
for 200-plus horsepower.

00:06:37.991 --> 00:06:39.700
The ratio is extreme.

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:41.260
And when they've got cars

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:42.859
that can just zoom right up to them,

00:06:42.859 --> 00:06:45.509
that's probably the most unrealistic part.

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:53.880
The ride into the traffic and
everything at the beginning

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:54.730
wasn't too bad.

00:06:54.730 --> 00:06:57.610
I think with two people
on it's unrealistic.

00:06:57.610 --> 00:06:59.640
You can't change direction that quickly.

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:01.470
Road bikes, the faster you go,

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:03.010
the harder it is to change direction.

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:05.731
If they're going at the
speeds that are implied,

00:07:05.731 --> 00:07:08.010
those maneuvers just aren't possible.

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:09.037
The stunt riders,

00:07:09.037 --> 00:07:10.820
they've got to have cars
coming along with them,

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:12.900
so they're restricted to
what speed they can do.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:14.040
And when they're going slower,

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:15.980
it's actually quite hard to have good form

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:17.880
as if you were doing those fast scenes.

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:20.840
So it's a very tricky thing to do.

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:21.981
And that's why I was impressed

00:07:21.981 --> 00:07:23.355
with the "Mission: Impossible,"

00:07:23.355 --> 00:07:27.400
because you're actually able to see

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:30.050
they've got quite good form
all through that scene.

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:32.070
They've got quite good styles
and everything like that.

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:35.610
So I would rate this a 3 or a 4 again.

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:38.485
The fact that they had a key,
I really liked that aspect.

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:48.798
Harleys in general are a
little difficult to ride.

00:07:48.798 --> 00:07:50.139
Everything's so low-slung,

00:07:50.139 --> 00:07:51.550
and it's quite actually hard

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:53.950
to get them to corner
at the best of times.

00:07:53.950 --> 00:07:56.790
Harleys actually can have
quite a lot of horsepower.

00:07:56.790 --> 00:07:59.109
I mean, they're incredible machines.

00:07:59.109 --> 00:08:03.225
As soon as you go down through
any kind of dip or curve,

00:08:03.225 --> 00:08:04.350
it's not going to like it.

00:08:09.590 --> 00:08:11.363
There's no way a big bike like that

00:08:11.363 --> 00:08:14.690
wouldn't just land and
go into a ton of pieces.

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:16.060
And if it was real, I mean,

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:18.210
I don't think they'd have
much of a back left, so.

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:22.190
That's quite hard for me
to see actually happening.

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:24.330
A big, heavy bike like that

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:25.800
landing from that sort of drop,

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:27.340
only a dirt bike could make that drop,

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:28.433
and it's still going to hurt a bit.

00:08:28.433 --> 00:08:30.816
I would say

00:08:30.816 --> 00:08:32.174
3, 4 again.

00:08:32.174 --> 00:08:34.884
There's parts of it that
were realistic enough.

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:41.130
Drifting on a motorcycle,

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:42.710
on a road bike's definitely possible.

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:44.620
To go that fast with that much lean angle,

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:48.950
that's basically an immediate
crash at those sort of speeds.

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:51.380
And, for me, at that angle
was just sort of not possible.

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:52.540
There's going to be no more grip,

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:54.950
and the bike will go
away from underneath you.

00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:56.237
There's lots of elements.

00:08:56.237 --> 00:08:59.497
Again, we're talking about
on the street as well.

00:08:59.497 --> 00:09:00.422
So if it was on a racetrack,

00:09:00.422 --> 00:09:03.629
you got consistent grip
levels, et cetera, et cetera.

00:09:03.629 --> 00:09:05.620
But this is the first time
seeing it on a street.

00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:06.900
There's oil spills, all that.

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:09.210
It's going to be very difficult to manage

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:10.360
and judge those situations.

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:12.685
So I would say that's nearly impossible.

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:19.210
That's not a problem to do
that and create a stoppie.

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:21.020
The distance that she did it in, though,

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:22.620
was probably unrealistic,

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:26.951
because to do a stoppie
actually makes you stop slower.

00:09:26.951 --> 00:09:28.605
If you've got the rear wheel, let's say,

00:09:28.605 --> 00:09:30.610
a foot off the ground, that's not too bad.

00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:32.510
That's at about your breaking limit,

00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:34.860
because there's a certain
force and angle and everything.

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:37.094
As soon as you start going too far,

00:09:37.094 --> 00:09:39.020
then that force is
carrying you over the top.

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:40.980
For someone to stop that
quick with a stoppie,

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:42.321
it's not realistic.

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:47.140
It's electric bike, so
they don't have clutches.

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:48.740
Just use the throttle
with an electric bike.

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:52.574
Which, again, would probably
make the burnout that she did

00:09:52.574 --> 00:09:53.800
quite difficult as well.

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:58.274
I would say, again, 3, 4 mark.

00:09:58.274 --> 00:09:59.937
Wai Lin: Give me that!

00:09:59.937 --> 00:10:01.479
Clutch, clutch, clutch!

00:10:01.479 --> 00:10:02.312
Turn right.

00:10:02.312 --> 00:10:03.392
No, left.

00:10:03.392 --> 00:10:04.259
Who's driving?

00:10:04.259 --> 00:10:06.680
Riding with two people
doing something different

00:10:06.680 --> 00:10:08.100
is nearly impossible.

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:09.550
It'd be easier to ride one-handed

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:13.200
than it would be to ride
with two people like that.

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:14.635
For two people to just jump on

00:10:14.635 --> 00:10:18.210
and one use the clutch
and the left handlebar

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:21.350
and one use the throttle
and brake on the right,

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:25.023
unless you've practiced day
in, day out with someone,

00:10:25.023 --> 00:10:27.440
to do something like that's,
you know, that's impossible.

00:10:30.044 --> 00:10:31.320
[bike engine buzzes]

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:33.069
The noise of this bike,

00:10:33.069 --> 00:10:34.527
there's no way a big bike like that's

00:10:34.527 --> 00:10:35.452
going to be that quiet.

00:10:35.452 --> 00:10:36.940
It sounded very much like a scooter.

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:38.543
So many scenes you'll see

00:10:38.543 --> 00:10:40.450
of two-strokes that
sound like a four-stroke.

00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:42.500
They just, you know, they
haven't quite got it right.

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:46.630
And this one almost sounds like
a scooter that's taking off.

00:10:46.630 --> 00:10:48.238
You know, it certainly
doesn't sound like a Harley

00:10:48.238 --> 00:10:50.195
or that big bike that
they're actually riding.

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:52.493
Bond: Hold on!

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:57.285
Doing a wheelie like that on that bike is,

00:10:57.285 --> 00:10:58.484
it's not impossible.

00:10:58.484 --> 00:10:59.580
Big, heavy bike like that,

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:01.552
you're going to struggle
to really get the power.

00:11:01.552 --> 00:11:04.558
It's long, and the balance
point's very, very,

00:11:04.558 --> 00:11:06.900
it's quite bad on those sort of bikes.

00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:08.240
You know, they're made
to stay on the ground.

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:09.943
They're not made to get the
front wheel in the air. So.

00:11:09.943 --> 00:11:12.830
I've seen people that can
wheelie just about anything,

00:11:12.830 --> 00:11:15.480
but, yeah, I would say, again,

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:18.320
that's a very, very low
probability to be able to do that.

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:20.310
Wheelies do not help you go faster.

00:11:20.310 --> 00:11:22.834
The amount of times I see them
drop back a couple of gears

00:11:22.834 --> 00:11:24.530
and [makes revving noise]
and then chuck a wheelie

00:11:24.530 --> 00:11:26.140
and all of a sudden they're flying along.

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:28.199
It's not faster.

00:11:28.199 --> 00:11:30.094
All of your weight then is going up.

00:11:30.094 --> 00:11:31.520
It's the same with the stoppie scene.

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:33.180
If you've got your weight going down,

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:34.880
it's wanting to overtake you.

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:37.290
But with a wheelie up
here, you can't accelerate.

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:39.050
You can't get going, because basically

00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:41.280
the bike wants to keep
going the other way.

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:44.996
So wheelies, especially
in racing, is our enemy.

00:11:44.996 --> 00:11:47.010
Occasionally, you can
come up and control it,

00:11:47.010 --> 00:11:50.578
but it has to be no more than
6, 8 inches off the ground.

00:11:50.578 --> 00:11:52.160
And that's where you find
that sort of balance point.

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:53.390
You get any further than that,

00:11:53.390 --> 00:11:54.980
it wants to start overtaking you,

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:56.883
and then you have to
start backing off, so.

00:11:56.883 --> 00:11:58.397
Unless you're throwing a wheelie,

00:11:58.397 --> 00:12:00.562
I guess, to stop some bullets hitting you,

00:12:00.562 --> 00:12:03.565
as we've seen in quite a few,

00:12:03.565 --> 00:12:06.690
yeah, there's no way
wheelies are helping you out.

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:15.360
Of course the jump is impossible,

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:17.640
and just going through
a roof and surviving it

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:19.880
and not breaking your
backs on just the impact.

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:22.970
That one was very, very
difficult to see the realism in.

00:12:22.970 --> 00:12:25.770
And I'd probably go a 1 or a 2.

00:12:34.585 --> 00:12:38.343
I know the rider who actually did that

00:12:38.343 --> 00:12:40.110
and ridden with him personally

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:43.120
and know that everything's
possible that's in that movie.

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:46.570
So, you know, there's nothing
unrealistic for me there.

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:49.460
The jump that he made up the
wall is definitely possible.

00:12:49.460 --> 00:12:52.330
He had, you know, small amount
of ramp there from the curb.

00:12:52.330 --> 00:12:54.830
The key to getting up heights like that,

00:12:54.830 --> 00:12:56.960
it's all loading the
body, loading the bike,

00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:59.450
loading the tires and suspension
all at the right moment.

00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:01.260
So they use that little pop, that ramp

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:02.690
for the front end to get that up,

00:13:02.690 --> 00:13:04.020
and to get over a wall like that,

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:05.680
you can't just clean jump it,

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:09.342
so basically they're
using their rear tire.

00:13:09.342 --> 00:13:10.660
Jumping up as far as they can,

00:13:10.660 --> 00:13:12.310
keep the front tire over the top of it,

00:13:12.310 --> 00:13:14.777
and then they use their rear
to ride up the last bit.

00:13:14.777 --> 00:13:17.121
And then that helps the front clear,

00:13:17.121 --> 00:13:19.367
and then they use their
momentum to get over, so.

00:13:19.367 --> 00:13:23.168
Everything that he did was 100% real,

00:13:23.168 --> 00:13:24.550
so 10 out of 10.

00:13:24.550 --> 00:13:26.960
They kept themselves in
a realistic state there,

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:28.177
so I like that one.

00:13:35.499 --> 00:13:37.150
Steve McQueen's an absolute legend

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:39.520
and I think did most of his
own stunts ever on a bike

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:40.353
and things like that.

00:13:40.353 --> 00:13:41.820
He was just incredible, you know,

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:44.390
there was no special effects
or anything like that.

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:48.200
That was a clean, honest
jump and everything.

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:49.300
Grass isn't too bad

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:52.532
if you've got off-road tires, knobbies.

00:13:52.532 --> 00:13:55.621
If they're fresh and have sharp edges,

00:13:55.621 --> 00:13:57.783
grass really isn't too bad to ride on.

00:13:57.783 --> 00:13:58.900
It gives you a fair bit of grip.

00:13:58.900 --> 00:14:01.710
But on the type of bike he
was on, with those tires,

00:14:01.710 --> 00:14:03.150
there wouldn't have been
a whole lot of grip.

00:14:03.150 --> 00:14:05.370
It was nice to see a couple
little slide-outs there,

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:06.695
things like that, because it was,

00:14:06.695 --> 00:14:07.903
you know, lush, green grass.

00:14:07.903 --> 00:14:10.270
It ends up being very, very slippery.

00:14:10.270 --> 00:14:12.620
But to be honest, quite a lot of fun.

00:14:12.620 --> 00:14:15.370
The jump, definitely possible.
Everything plausible.

00:14:15.370 --> 00:14:16.887
So, yeah, I'd have to give it a 10.

00:14:22.228 --> 00:14:23.973
The sparking boots is something

00:14:23.973 --> 00:14:25.760
that they would have made or designed.

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:28.416
It could just be a few screws

00:14:28.416 --> 00:14:30.050
that they put around
the edge of the boots.

00:14:30.050 --> 00:14:32.948
We started to do a night race in Qatar,

00:14:32.948 --> 00:14:35.740
and during the night race,
when I was racing Ducati,

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:38.150
we had quite low foot pegs

00:14:38.150 --> 00:14:40.270
the way our bike was
designed and everything.

00:14:40.270 --> 00:14:42.490
So during the daytime you
couldn't see it sparking,

00:14:42.490 --> 00:14:45.199
but at nighttime, because I
was touching the foot pegs

00:14:45.199 --> 00:14:47.192
on one of the corners on the track,

00:14:47.192 --> 00:14:48.360
it would spark quite a lot.

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:50.350
It's not too difficult to get

00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:52.217
some sparks happening at nighttime.

00:15:00.820 --> 00:15:03.895
The wheelies were all possible.

00:15:03.895 --> 00:15:05.200
The stoppies were possible.

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:06.410
It's all more than plausible.

00:15:06.410 --> 00:15:08.130
It's all stuff I can do myself,

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:09.871
so if I can do it,

00:15:09.871 --> 00:15:11.762
definitely there's some people out there

00:15:11.762 --> 00:15:13.140
that can do a heck of
a lot more than that.

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:15.464
The one thing that I probably couldn't do

00:15:15.464 --> 00:15:18.500
was when he did the handstand,

00:15:18.500 --> 00:15:22.103
they only used the clutch when
they were going back gears.

00:15:22.103 --> 00:15:23.220
They weren't going too fast.

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:27.600
Doing everything in every
maneuver they did was plausible.

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:29.760
Everything's realistic there.

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:31.660
I'd have to say, yeah, 10 out of 10.

00:15:32.530 --> 00:15:34.396
If you enjoyed this video,

00:15:34.396 --> 00:15:36.175
click here to watch another one.

